WEBVTT

00:00:02.708 --> 00:00:03.375
As demand

00:00:03.375 --> 00:00:06.375
for AI
grows, so does the need for compute.

00:00:06.416 --> 00:00:09.416
But raw power isn’t enough if it’s siloed.

00:00:09.541 --> 00:00:13.250
Old data center designs used to focus
on separating computer systems,

00:00:13.583 --> 00:00:16.250
treating each server as a standalone unit.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.375
This made them inefficient
for the connected needs of modern AI.

00:00:20.166 --> 00:00:23.666
To scale AI effectively,
we need a full-stack cluster-level

00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:27.333
view of the system, and to uplevel
the unit of design and deployment.

00:00:27.916 --> 00:00:31.625
And yet it must also be modular,
interoperable infrastructure

00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:34.625
that connects everything
from silicon to data center.

00:00:34.791 --> 00:00:39.000
In other words, we need to see the forest
and the trees, and co-design

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.791
the smaller details with the whole system
in mind as well as its evolution.

00:00:43.791 --> 00:00:47.416
The OCP’s Open Systems
for AI initiative builds that vision.

00:00:47.416 --> 00:00:52.791
With community-driven designs
like ORv3 racks, OAI modules, NVIDIA’s

00:00:52.791 --> 00:00:58.250
contributed MGX, and Meta’s contributed
Catalina, OCP is laying the foundation

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.625
for vendor-neutral, high-performance
AI clusters.

00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:04.875
At the cluster level, compute is organized

00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:09.833
into pods—compact, GPU-dense
groups of servers linked by high-speed

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:14.500
fabrics like NVLink, UALink, and Scale-Up
Ethernet.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:20.500
These interconnects enable parallelism
at scale, growing up to 1,024 accelerators

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.083
operating as a unit of deployment

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:26.375
and unified
high-performance system for AI.

00:01:26.458 --> 00:01:32.333
Take the GB200-NVL72
rack—a dense OCP-aligned pod

00:01:32.541 --> 00:01:35.291
that draws over 120 kilowatts.

00:01:35.291 --> 00:01:40.041
These systems push the limits of power
and thermal design, requiring advanced

00:01:40.041 --> 00:01:44.750
liquid cooling and highly efficient
layouts—enabled by OCP standards.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.041
And hardware is just the beginning.

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:51.291
OCP drives full-stack enablement—from
firmware and board management

00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:52.250
to abstraction layers

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.333
and APIs—ensuring performance, resilience,
and platform interoperability.

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:00.625
So, it isn't just one component, one

00:02:00.625 --> 00:02:03.625
chip,
or one pod that can make the difference.

00:02:03.625 --> 00:02:06.125
We need to learn
and think in terms of the system

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:09.083
view and design interdependencies. 
Power delivery.

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:10.583
Cooling distribution.

00:02:10.583 --> 02:13.541
Interconnect
fabrics. System orchestration.

00:02:13.541 --> 00:02:17.541
Built with openness
and interoperation together, they define

00:02:17.541 --> 00:02:21.458
a scalable architecture designed
for the compute demands of modern AI.

00:02:21.458 --> 00:02:25.375
Open Systems for AI turns complexity
into capability—one open

00:02:25.375 --> 00:02:27.750
building block at a time.